  "text": "**Solutions 1 and 2 are wrong.** Solution 1 gives $c=1$ with no real derivation. Solution 2 finds that $c\\ge 3$ is needed for the bound to refine $2m-3$, then reports $c=2$ anyway, which contradicts its own requirement.\n\n**Solution 3 gives $c=3$, but its final step doesn't support that value.** Its geometric step ($d \\ge n-m+2$) is asserted without full justification, so I can't check that part. Taking its premises at face value:\n\n- The rank-metric Singleton bound for $n\\ge m$ is $km \\le n(m-d+1)$.\n- With $d\\ge n-m+2$, this gives $km \\le n(2m-1-n)$.\n- Writing $n=2m-j$ gives $km \\le (2m-j)(j-1)$.\n- Since $2m-j<2m$, this forces $k < 2(j-1)$ strictly, so $j \\ge \\lfloor k/2\\rfloor + 2 = \\lfloor (k+4)/2\\rfloor$.\n\nThe solution instead rounds to $j \\ge \\lceil k/2\\rceil+1$, which is weaker for even $k$. Its claim that $c=3$ \"coincides with $2m-3$ for $k=3,4$\" fails under its own inequality: for $k=4$, $j=3$ requires $4m \\le 4m-6$, which is impossible. So its own chain points to $c=4$, not $c=3$.\n\nI could not verify the source paper's constant, so I can't rule out that the intended answer is 3. But Solution 3's argument, as written, does not establish it.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
